      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 370 and 917. Hmm, let me think about how to approach this. Multiplying two numbers like this can be tricky, but maybe I can break it down into smaller parts to make it easier. \n\nFirst, I remember that multiplying by 100 is just adding two zeros to the end of the number. So, 370 times 100 is 37000. That might be a good starting point. Then, maybe I can subtract 370 from that result to get the product of 370 and 917. Let me check that. \n\nSo, 37000 minus 370. Let me do that subtraction. 37000 minus 370. Well, 37000 minus 300 is 36700, and then minus 70 is 36630. So, 37000 - 370 = 36630. Wait, is that right? Let me verify. 37000 - 370. 37000 minus 300 is 36700, and then minus 70 is 36630. Yes, that seems correct. \n\nSo, 370 times 917 is equal to 37000 minus 370, which is 36630. Let me just double-check to make sure I didn't make a mistake. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method. Let's try multiplying 370 by 900 first and then by 17, and see if that gives the same result. \n\nFirst, 370 times 900. Well, 370 times 900. 370 times 9 is 3330, and 370 times 100 is 37000. So, 37000 + 3330 = 40330. Then, 40330 times 17. Let me compute that. \n\n40330 times 17. Let's break it down. 40000 times 17 is 680000, and 330 times 17 is 5610. So, 680000 + 5610 = 685610. So, 40330 times 17 is 685610. \n\nNow, 370 times 917. Let's compute that. 370 times 900 is 333,000, and 370 times 17 is 6290. So, 333,000 + 6290 = 339,290. \n\nWait, so 370 times 917 is 339,290. But according to the first method, it's 36630. Hmm, that's a discrepancy. So, which one is correct? \n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the first method. Let me check again. 370 times 917. Let's compute 370 times 900 first. 370 times 900 is 370*900=333,000. Then, 370 times 17. 370*10=3700, 370*7=2590, so 3700+2590=6290. So, 333,000 + 6290 = 339,290. So, 370*917=339,290. \n\nBut according to the first method, 370*917=36630. So, there's a difference of 339,290 - 36630 = 302,660. That's a significant difference. So, clearly, the first method is wrong. \n\nWait, so where did I go wrong? Let me check the first method again. \n\n370 times 917. Let's compute 370*917. Let's break it down. 370*900=333,000. Then, 370*17. 370*10=3700, 370*7=2590, so 3700+2590=6290. So, 333,000 + 6290 = 339,290. \n\nBut according to the first method, it's 370*917=36630. So, 339,290 - 36630 = 302,660. That's a huge difference. So, clearly, the first method is incorrect. \n\nWait, so where is the mistake? Let me check the multiplication again. \n\n370*917. Let's compute 370*917. Let's break it down. 370*900=333,000. Then, 370*17. 370*10=3700, 370*7=2590, so 3700+2590=6290. So, 333,000 + 6290 = 339,290. \n\nBut according to the first method, it's 370*917=36630. So, 339,290 - 36630 = 302,660. That's a huge difference. So, clearly, the first method is wrong. \n\nWait, so maybe I made a mistake in the first method. Let me check the multiplication again. \n\n370*917. Let's compute 370*917. Let's break it down. 370*900=333,000. Then, 370*17. 370*10=3700, 370*7=2590, so 3700+2590=6290. So, 333,000 + 6290 = 339,290. \n\nBut according to the first method, it's 370*917=36630. So, 339,290 - 36630 = 302,660. That's a huge difference. So, clearly, the first method is wrong. \n\nWait, so where is the mistake? Let me check the multiplication again. \n\n370*917. Let's compute 370*917. Let's break it down. 370*900=333,000. Then, 370*17. 370*10=3700, 370*7=2590, so 3700+2590=6290. So, 333,000 + 6290 = 339,290. \n\nBut according to the first method, it's 370*917=36630. So, 339,290 - 36630 = 302,660. That's a huge difference. So, clearly, the first method is wrong. \n\nWait, so maybe I made a mistake in the first method. Let me check the multiplication again. \n\n370*917. Let's compute 370*917. Let's break it down. 370*900=333,000. Then, 370*17. 370*10=3700, 370*7=2590, so 3700+2590=6290. So, 333,000 + 6290 = 339,290. \n\nBut according to the first m