      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 280 and 259. Hmm, let me think about how to approach this. Multiplying two numbers can sometimes be tricky, but maybe I can break it down into smaller parts to make it easier. \n\nFirst, let's recall that multiplying two numbers can be done by adding one number to itself as many times as the other number. But since these numbers are quite large, maybe I should use the standard multiplication method. Let me write them out:\n\n280\n\u00d7 259\n\nI remember that when multiplying, you can align the numbers vertically and multiply each digit of the second number by each digit of the first number, starting from the right. Then, you add up all the products. Let me try that step by step.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit of 259, which is 9. Multiply 280 by 9. Let's do that first. 280 times 9. Hmm, 280 times 10 is 2800, and 280 times 9 is 2520. So adding those together gives 2800 + 2520 = 5320. That's the rightmost digit of the product.\n\nNow, moving to the next digit, which is 5. Multiply 280 by 5. Let's see, 280 times 5. 280 times 5 is 1400. So that's the middle digit of the product. Then, we have to add a zero at the end because we're multiplying by the next digit. So 1400 + 0 = 1400. Wait, but hold on, the original numbers are 280 and 259. So the second number is 259, which has two digits. So when multiplying, we need to consider the second number as 259, which is 259. So the multiplication is 280 \u00d7 259. Let me check that again.\n\nWait, actually, when multiplying 280 by 259, the second number is 259. So 280 \u00d7 259. Let me verify that. 280 \u00d7 259. Let's break it down. 280 \u00d7 250 = 280 \u00d7 250 = 70,000. Then, 280 \u00d7 9 = 2520. So 70,000 + 2520 = 72,520. So the product is 72,520. But wait, let me confirm that again. Maybe I made a mistake in the multiplication.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method to verify. Let's try the standard multiplication method again. Let's write 280 and 259 vertically:\n\n  280\n\u00d7 259\n--------\n\nFirst, multiply 280 by 9 (the rightmost digit of 259). 280 \u00d7 9 = 2520. Then, multiply 280 by 5 (the next digit). 280 \u00d7 5 = 1400. Then, since there's a zero in the middle, we add another zero to the end. So 1400 + 0 = 1400. Then, add the two results together: 2520 + 1400 = 3920. Wait, but that's different from the previous result. Wait, that can't be right. Wait, hold on, maybe I made a mistake in the multiplication.\n\nWait, let me check again. Let's do the multiplication step by step. 280 \u00d7 259. Let's break it down:\n\n280 \u00d7 259 = 280 \u00d7 (200 + 50 + 9) = 280 \u00d7 200 + 280 \u00d7 50 + 280 \u00d7 9.\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n280 \u00d7 200 = 56,000\n\n280 \u00d7 50 = 14,000\n\n280 \u00d7 9 = 2,520\n\nAdding them up: 56,000 + 14,000 = 70,000; 70,000 + 2,520 = 72,520. So the product is 72,520. But wait, that's different from the previous result. Wait, but that can't be right. Wait, maybe I made a mistake in the multiplication.\n\nWait, let me check the multiplication again. Let's take 280 \u00d7 259. Let's do it step by step:\n\n280 \u00d7 259 = 280 \u00d7 (200 + 50 + 9) = 280 \u00d7 200 + 280 \u00d7 50 + 280 \u00d7 9.\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n280 \u00d7 200 = 56,000\n\n280 \u00d7 50 = 14,000\n\n280 \u00d7 9 = 2,520\n\nAdding them up: 56,000 + 14,000 = 70,000; 70,000 + 2,520 = 72,520. So the product is 72,520. But wait, that's different from the previous result. Wait, but that can't be right. Wait, maybe I made a mistake in the multiplication.\n\nWait, let me check the multiplication again. Let's take 280 \u00d7 259. Let's do it step by step:\n\n280 \u00d7 259 = 280 \u00d7 (200 + 50 + 9) = 280 \u00d7 200 + 280 \u00d7 50 + 280 \u00d7 9.\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n280 \u00d7 200 = 56,000\n\n280 \u00d7 50 = 14,000\n\n280 \u00d7 9 = 2,520\n\nAdding them up: 56,000 + 14,000 = 70,000; 70,000 + 2,520 = 72,520. So the product is 72,520. But wait, that's different from th